packages/contracts test: ▶ range-check corpus
packages/contracts test:   ✔ the corpus is non-trivial and covers both verdicts (1.6064ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ every distinct violation code is exercised at least once (0.3774ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ in-bounds-mid-asset — The ordinary case: a range comfortably inside the asset. (1.3365ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ ends-exactly-at-duration — The range is half-open, so end == duration consumes the whole asset and reads nothing past it. An off-by-one here would reject every full-length range. (0.2455ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ minimal-one-tick-range — The smallest legal range. Proves the non-empty rule is > and not >=. (0.176ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ one-tick-past-the-end — The exit criterion in miniature. A single tick past the duration is the defect that reaches a render as a black frame or a decode error. (0.391ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ starts-at-the-duration — A range beginning where the asset ends contains no media at all. (0.2306ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ empty-range — endTicks == startTicks. JSON Schema provably cannot express this (draft 2020-12 has no cross-property inequality and tech-spec §3 forbids if/then/else), so it can only be caught here. (0.2124ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ inverted-range — endTicks < startTicks. Same unexpressible inequality, opposite direction. (0.2112ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ audio-timebase-exactly-full-length — 48 kHz ticks ARE sample counts. 480000 samples == 10 s == the whole 300-tick @30fps asset. Cross-timebase comparison must be exact. (0.3474ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ audio-timebase-one-sample-over — A SINGLE sample past the end, across differing timebases. This is the case a float comparison silently passes — the reason the implementation cross-multiplies with BigInt. (1.3878ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ wrong-asset — A range is only meaningful against its own asset; checking it against another's duration would be a coincidence, not a validation. (0.2221ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ negative-start — Ticks are counts and cannot be negative. (0.1205ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ fractional-tick — A fractional tick is a category error — it means someone did float-seconds arithmetic upstream, which is exactly what the rational timebase exists to prevent. (0.1665ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ malformed-range-missing-ticks — The untrusted path this module exists for: a model-proposed range that is not structurally a range at all. A missing tick is a STRUCTURAL defect, not a fractional one — reporting NON_INTEGER_TICKS would send the reader hunting for float arithmetic that was never involved. (0.2167ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ unsafe-integer-ticks — Above 2^53 the value was destroyed by JSON.parse before any comparison ran, so an out-of-bounds range was reported clean. The checker refuses an unrepresentable tick rather than comparing a number it knows is already wrong. (0.155ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ zero-denominator-timebase — Must be reported as an invalid timebase, never reach a division. A validator that divides by zero cannot be run over generated output. (0.1473ms)
packages/contracts test:   ✔ unknown-duration-fails-closed — An asset whose preflight established no duration gives no bound to prove against. The only safe verdict is refusal — passing would let an unbounded range through on the strength of missing information. (0.205ms)
